{"title":"1996 AHSME Problems/Problem 27","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(minimal_3D_geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (minimal 3D geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider two solid spherical balls, one centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b483aed95e9b4ab2d874209a2fbd1c3cdec42a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(0, 0,\\frac{21}{2}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"79\" height=\"43\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the other centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/a/87aa41dd3a801fda3ea956ee4a4570169dc07955.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adcd622a1ba9d557e1e14b7810cd5852e5ec05e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. How many points with only integer coordinates (lattice points) are there in the intersection of the balls? \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/b/83bd949513c194d8d50f9dde5524d405709e3ab9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 7\\qquad\\text{(B)}\\ 9\\qquad\\text{(C)}\\ 11\\qquad\\text{(D)}\\ 13\\qquad\\text{(E)}\\ 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"357\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The two equations of the balls are\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/d/05d38c791a9a4ff1eed17200cb427f9367e0230e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + y^2 + \\left(z - \\frac{21}{2}\\right)^2 \\le 36\\]\" width=\"207\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/b/54b478de5cf20da9b1d5cc509351834c2c69d668.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + y^2 + (z - 1)^2 \\le \\frac{81}{4}\\]\" width=\"185\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Note that along the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> axis, the first ball goes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/2/ea203e55b79f0c6b00a629cf308919b9d6c2fa39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10.5 \\pm 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"63\" height=\"13\" />, and the second ball goes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c7b3cf3ff776cd019ba4323f8186d025650afd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\pm 4.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"54\" height=\"13\" />.  The only integer value that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> can be is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/5/0a55a1e3a4ae7aeeabbb5d6190332ed73cfe2a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z=5$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Plugging that in to both equations, we get:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/a/c6acb0118bd08e6796e64602521f8d40b48a41e3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + y^2 \\le \\frac{23}{4}\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/4/e64df4289bec71e1c500aa3408416be49f57a41d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + y^2 \\le \\frac{17}{4}\\]\" width=\"101\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>The second inequality implies the first inequality, so the only condition that matters is the second inequality.\n</p><p>From here, we do casework, noting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d88475b9b606e651a3a4cd0d992742a67a7998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x|, |y| \\le 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" />:\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/8/fa8104cf09287e76de493e9cbed38fcf3d9db186.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\pm 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" />, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />.  This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> points.\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/4/4f424f1753ba66a66c48a233c5afe322d41467a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\pm 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" />, we can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/c/36cccdbb1c791846916a28b1b0a7c16c1e21c804.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y\\in \\{-1, 0, 1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" />.  This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/e/51e4c3eea0ed69c36ca84ffb61aa8380a52555cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot 3 = 6$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" /> points.\n</p><p>For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/3/2d348bde3e15456e71734dc2c56fc7425c95927f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, we can have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/5/42570083a121c72c6d1657fad9c5aaa87519a686.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y \\in \\{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"18\" />.  This gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> points.\n</p><p>Thus, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e40bc2b9bcef49b195e9be1e88febb6eff5fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" /> possible points, giving answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/b/55bdade53f471aab49f63d1a734979cc0c636640.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{D}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"29\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28minimal_3D_geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(minimal_3D_geometry)\">Solution 2 (minimal 3D geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (minimal 3D geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because both spheres have their centers on the x-axis, we can simplify the graph a bit by looking at a 2-dimensional plane (the previous z-axis is the new x-axis while the y-axis remains the same).\n</p><p>The spheres now become circles with centers at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d323a9222b9c1f64be2e61dc2bece95f2d2cc4cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{21}{2},0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"52\" height=\"37\" />.  They have radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adcd622a1ba9d557e1e14b7810cd5852e5ec05e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.\nLet circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the circle centered on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the one centered on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d323a9222b9c1f64be2e61dc2bece95f2d2cc4cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{21}{2},0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"52\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>The point on circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> closest to the center of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/5/2e5b4ecd55d11e261c3008670d2eab9ba77639a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{11}{2},0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"52\" height=\"37\" />.  The point on circle B closest to the center of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/5/2e5b4ecd55d11e261c3008670d2eab9ba77639a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{11}{2},0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"52\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Taking a look back at the 3-dimensional coordinate grid with the spheres, we can see that their intersection appears to be a circle with congruent \"dome\" shapes on either end.  Because the tops of the \"domes\" are at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8337f84f92454e35783480f343d6fd195594b830.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,\\frac{9}{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"60\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/8/568e85fb6949d27aab5abfd59315337f28aadf9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,\\frac{11}{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"69\" height=\"37\" />, respectively, the lattice points inside the area of intersection must have z-value <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the only integer between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adcd622a1ba9d557e1e14b7810cd5852e5ec05e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/a/53a36e5a7787e442ab3272b7417996dd0c372b24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{11}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />).  Thus, the lattice points in the area of intersection must all be on the 2-dimensional circle.  The radius of the circle will be the distance from the z-axis.\n</p><p>Now, looking at the 2-dimensional coordinate plane, we see that the radius of the circle (now the distance from the x-axis, because there is no more z-axis) is the altitude of a triangle with two points on centers of circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and third point at the first quadrant intersection of the circles.  Let's call that altitude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We know all three side lengths of this triangle:  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adcd622a1ba9d557e1e14b7810cd5852e5ec05e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> (the radius of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> (the radius of circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />), and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c57b9cfb8dc9067067ef658ff6845a667b594a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{19}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> (the distance between the centers of circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />).  We can now find the area of the triangle using Heron's formula:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/c/aac663dde3d4bd6356a1feb6d09ad7d6bbe1777c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s=\\frac{\\frac{9}{2}+6+\\frac{19}{2}}{2}=20\\]\" width=\"159\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/8/748a2b4f847ef16f15b7e024fbaa306f68888874.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A=\\sqrt{(20)(20-\\frac{9}{2})(20-6)(20-\\frac{19}{2})}=\\sqrt{110}\\]\" width=\"362\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Using the area of the triangle, we can find that altitude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> from the x-axis:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/2/8c2719a92e067a786096dfd74c0972114b8401dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{110}=\\frac{h*\\frac{19}{2}}{2}\\]\" width=\"113\" height=\"40\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/5/f95c4860d353743dc8bdabb398f115de8e05e70b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[h=\\frac{4*\\sqrt{110}}{19}\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Remember, the altitude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is also the radius of the circle containing all the solutions to the problem.\n</p><p>Going back to the 3-dimensional grid and looking at the circle, we can again make the figure 2-dimensional.\n</p><p>The radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/8/8189a5b5a0917b8c93350827be4038af1839139d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> of the circle in a 2-dimensional plane is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/5/315016a81b8eec02f2a23d90fca319427bbb0418.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4\\sqrt{110}}{19}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"57\" height=\"43\" />, a little greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.  We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/2/4b22ade43a200a367eefedb1791d979d4bca47b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/9/f5900c3664d14a3acff2c4f7273e08a30ef55e49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\sqrt{110}&gt;4*10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/6/16601a78645e57b9460aefbcab77563d34cf982c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{40}{19}&gt;2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, looking at a circle with radius slightly larger than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we see that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e40bc2b9bcef49b195e9be1e88febb6eff5fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" /> lattice points within it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/b/55bdade53f471aab49f63d1a734979cc0c636640.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{D}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"29\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the spheres are on the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> axis. Therefore, we can draw the spheres so that only the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />  and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> axis are featured. \n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:1996AHSMEP271.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f2/1996AHSMEP271.jpg/300px-1996AHSMEP271.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"375\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f2/1996AHSMEP271.jpg/450px-1996AHSMEP271.jpg 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/f/f2/1996AHSMEP271.jpg/600px-1996AHSMEP271.jpg 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/8/7b8074e3b909a05c3a15acbf334f0cd43277585d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = (1, 0, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62ed459a26b8b166d8ed1d80c013d56946a9017c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1^2+4^2} = \\sqrt{17}&lt;\\frac92$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"167\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the smaller sphere. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/0/7d0c6aaebe6c8c9e54512ab363f9ba0476aa5b0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{1^2+(\\frac{11}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{126}}{2}&lt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"206\" height=\"45\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the larger sphere. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the intersection.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/c/8ccfce6463e422d7d50ca95cfc1c70a28b0ad792.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B = (2, 0, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299365d334fa503d6953ac1bc632cb4da205ed58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2^2+4^2} = \\sqrt{20}&lt;\\frac92$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"167\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the smaller sphere. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/5/5e54b4f064b6ce183d8c8410412898e25f2eb365.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2^2+(\\frac{11}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{137}}{2}&lt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"206\" height=\"45\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the larger sphere. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the intersection.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/a/14a02f9e7a8f556d23006ee7db9d3c199560a5a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C = (3, 0, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/e/a0e7424d54575b0577e0bc8035cf7537988e3d25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = \\sqrt{25}&gt;\\frac92$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"167\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is outside the smaller sphere. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/2/e922aba1bff89c4053cc7859723435d862778531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3^2+(\\frac{11}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{157}}{2}&gt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"206\" height=\"45\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is outside the larger sphere. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is outside the intersection.\n</p><p>The intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> spheres is a circle, by drawing the circle flat we can see that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more points within the intersection. \n</p><p>We can see that points with the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinates with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are still inside the circle. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is very close to the circle, the points with the same <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinates with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are not necessarily inside the circle.  \n</p><p>For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/1/fa10a2391e3407fe4b919514e2a3c64618c64399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D = (2,1,5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/8/6b896ab389008a868405e7786ebb5b3c36d73fec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2^2+1^2+4^2}=\\sqrt{21}&gt;\\frac92$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"206\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is outside the smaller sphere. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/3/1e3dfcfd20cea703c8074eb30360cd6ff3a9aae2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{2^2 + 1^2 + (\\frac{11}{2})^2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{141}}{2}&lt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"245\" height=\"45\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is inside the larger sphere. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is outside the intersection.\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:1996AHSMEP272.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/77/1996AHSMEP272.jpg/200px-1996AHSMEP272.jpg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/77/1996AHSMEP272.jpg/300px-1996AHSMEP272.jpg 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/77/1996AHSMEP272.jpg/400px-1996AHSMEP272.jpg 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/f/57f8fa3129a66dded19e43042610822b0bf4d3a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9+4 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"132\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Isabelchen\">isabelchen</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_27&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME\" title=\"1996 AHSME\">1996 AHSME</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1996 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1996 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1996\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1996_AHSME_Problems/Problem_26\" title=\"1996 AHSME Problems/Problem 26\"